! conclusion:
  Every participant in the loop — operator, agent, stakeholder —
  receives the feedback that changes their behavior, as a typed
  channel with a named acceptance side. Learning that dies at an
  edge is the loop's most expensive silent failure.

  + context:
    Operator turns dominate the lifecycle; agent discoveries do not
    teach humans; stakeholder families (UKRN trainers) have no
  feedback path at all; and understanding that does not update the
    argument is motion, not learning.

  + IF:
    Any participant's state should have changed because of what
    another participant learned.

  + HOWEVER:
    Attention is the scarcest resource on the human edges: feedback
    must be priced (the ask arm's cost), batched at the right
    cadence (constellations, not per-turn noise), and routed to
    where it can act — not broadcast.

  + THEN:
    Each edge is a channel by the four-field test (payload,
    acceptance authority, reconciliation, budget); discoveries that
    should teach a human surface as typed reports with their
    evidence; stakeholder boards carry their own per-author
    precision rows; the edge that cannot yet carry feedback is a
    named gap with an owner.

  + BECAUSE:
    A loop that learns on its machine edges and not its human edges
    optimizes itself away from the people it serves — and the apex
    thesis's "community of humans and machines" fails at exactly the
    boundary this pattern governs.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Audit the seven lifecycle stages for feedback edges; name the dead ones.]
    next[The Rebecca board is this pattern's first stakeholder instance when built.]
